{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787151875,"nonce":"N09/U7Mnuw+ZdpJiLw0IXR+J818=","message":"**Done in `522bef5` and `9761298`, and the count in the title undercounts by two.** Twenty-two sites, not twenty: the sweep that filed this ticket missed one, and I missed one more that a red team found in the file I had edited most.\n\n**The three that were not cosmetic, all fixed:**\n\n`tools/lintvocab/main.go:100` — the violation message for the still-banned word `escalation` ended *\"Say unacked_reminder.\"* `lint-vocabulary` reports 60 violations, so this fires today, and a developer following it renames to a term that exists nowhere. The rule stays, because `CONTEXT.md:59` says *\"`escalation` remains a banned word\"*; only the remedy changed, and it now says there is no replacement, which is the actual ruling. Verified after the change that the regex, the term list and the scanned roots are byte-identical and the count is still exactly 60 across 4 terms — the message moved, the behaviour did not.\n\n`CONTEXT.md` contradicted itself 48 lines apart: `:59` records the withdrawal, `:107`'s vocabulary list said *\"Say **unacked reminder**, not escalation.\"*\n\n⭐ **SPEC §L.5.1 was labelled \"literal\" and was not — and the sweep reported this one as \"since fully resolved\", which was wrong.** Its Slack channel-config block declared `mention_on_reminder` between `max_instances` and `link_names`; the real `internal/channels/providers/slack/schema.json` goes straight from one to the other. An operator writing config from the SPEC would have had that key rejected by `DisallowUnknownFields` with no hint why. I found it only because I checked the claim instead of taking it.\n\n⛔ **And then I made the same mistake one layer up.** After deleting `mention_on_reminder` I wrote that the block \"now matches the file\". A red team refuted it in one command: `transport` still diverged three ways — the SPEC gave `\"title\": \"Interactivity transport\"` with `\"default\": \"socket_mode\"` and no `description`, where the real file titles it *\"(deprecated, ignored)\"*, carries a long description saying Socket Mode is not implemented at all, and has **no `default` key**. So a \"literal\" block was advertising a default for a field the implementation ignores. Both blocks are now compared by **parsing** — property sets identical, every property's JSON equal — and the SPEC tells the next editor to parse and diff rather than eyeball. That instruction is the fix; the `transport` text is today's instance of it.\n\n**Site twenty-two, also found by the red team:** ADR 0020's Amendment 4 has a \"What does not change\" section, written 2026-08-14, still reading *\"The broadcast set (Amendment 1): `unacked_reminder` and `refired`\"* and *\"The mention placement (Amendment 3). The mention stays in the top-level `text`\"* — present tense, unmarked. The file's top-of-page callout, Amendment 3's heading and rule 5b had all been corrected; this section had not. ⛔ It also falsified a sentence I had just written eleven lines below it in Amendment 5 (*\"Nothing in Amendments 1-4 is reversed\"* — Amendment 3 is reversed in full). One bullet of the three was struck through rather than deleted, because Amendment 3's justification for the top-level `text` — *\"the only position that reaches a push notification\"* — is the contrast that explains why `68653ca` put a LINK in the body instead: a mention's value IS the notification, a link's value is being clickable.\n\n**Everything else**, each read before it was edited: `renderer.go:36` (asserted `RenderOptions.Mentions` one paragraph below the correct ⛔ in the same block) · `mode.go:280` (`PlanFor` described as three ordered steps beginning with the reminder, while the body's own ⛔ says that step is deleted — renumbered to two, with the departure called out, because the reminder was the only UNREDUCIBLE step) · `ports.go:148` · `channel.go:88` · `adapters.go:737` · `orgs.go:54` (a paragraph about \"every one of these\" with no fields after it) · `mapper.go:13,51` · `reply.go:68` and `:699`, plus `text := sentence`, the dead alias whose only purpose was the mention · three `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` DESCRIPTIONS with `web/src/api/generated/` REGENERATED, never hand-edited · `deploy/slack/manifest.yaml` · `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md` · `docs/adr/0013` · `docs/design/notification-content-customisation.md` · `docs/runbooks/oto_ingest_process_duration_seconds.md` · `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts`.\n\n**Two sites needed a product call and I made it** rather than leaving them silently: both described *\"the other two\"* fixed broadcasts, and the honest set is awkward because `refired` has no producer (`broadcast.go:71`). Operator-facing text now says `broadcast_on_resolved` is the only broadcast oto can currently send, instead of enumerating a dormant Reason as though it fires. One edit each to reverse.\n\n**The OpenAPI edits were verified DESCRIPTION-ONLY structurally, not by eye:** with every `description:` key stripped and the contract canonicalised, the diff against `4f5204f` is zero lines — no `required`, `enum`, `type`, `maxItems`, `properties`, path or operation moved. So no breaking-change declaration is owed. `validators.ts` needed no regeneration, and both generators reproduce the checked-in files exactly.\n\n**Gate:** the eleven `ci` recipes at `9761298`, `dirty_before 0`/`dirty_after 0`. Nine green; `lint-vocabulary` exactly 60 — unchanged by a sweep that touched both `internal/**` and `web/src`, the two scanned roots — and exactly one `--- FAIL`, `TestNoStateHueInChrome`. Zero drift from the pre-flight baseline.\n\n⛔ **One remediation round, and the gate caught it:** deleting a dead mock method left a double blank line and `lint` failed on `gofmt`. `go build` and `go vet` had both passed on that file. Three checks green and the one that mattered was not among them.\n\n⚠️ **`docs-site/**` deliberately untouched** — a generated mirror of `docs/`, last synced the day before the deletion, so most of it still describes the reminder as live. Sync lag in a mirror, not hand-authored prose. It is the largest remaining body of stale text about this feature and running the generator is the whole fix.\n\n⚠️ **Not pushed**, and the git-bug store needs its own `git-bug push origin`.","files":null}]}